ddot; No Blooket</div>\n 119:   </div>\n 120:   <div class=\"meta\" id=\"dateLabel\"></div>\n 121: </header>\n 122: \n 123: <nav class=\"tabs\" id=\"tabBar\">\n 124:   <button class=\"tab active\" data-tab=\"p1\">P1 &middot; Radical Equations (Foundation)</button>\n 125:   <button class=\"tab\" data-tab=\"p2\">P2 &middot; Rational Exponents + DOK-3 &#11088;</button>\n 126:   <button class=\"tab\" data-tab=\"p3\">P3 &middot; Solve for Variable &middot; Assessment-Critical &#11088;</button>\n 127: </nav>\n 128: \n 129: <main id=\"panels\"></main>\n 130: \n 131: <script>\n 132: const PACER = {\n 133:   p1: {\n 134:     label: \"Period 1 \u00b7 Solving Radical Equations \u2014 Foundation \u00b7 55 min base (65 min A & F)\",\n 135:     total_min: 55,\n 136:     phases: [\n 137:       { id:\"donow\", title:\"Do Now \u2014 Notice & Wonder (Explore & Reason)\",\n 138:         fw:\"exploration\", dok:\"2\", min:5,\n 139:         items:[{qid:\"5-4-savvas-model-discuss-lesson-5-4-launch\", label:\"Model & Discuss \u2014 Explore & Reason (Parts A/B/C)\"}],\n 140:         teacher:[\n 141:           \"Project the starter (or use printed Do Now sheet).\",\n 142:           \"Circulate SILENTLY \u2014 do not teach during this phase.\",\n 143:           \"Collect at 5 min sharp.\"\n 144:         ],\n 145:         rules:[\n 146:           \"A. Solve 3(a+1)\u00b2 + 2 = 11 using at least two methods.\",\n 147:           \"B. Try each method on 3\u221a(a+1) + 2 = 11.\",\n 148:           \"C. Generalize: which method is better suited for equations with a radical? What problems arise from the other?\"\n 149:         ],\n 150:         answer:\"NOTICE (typical): 'There's a square root.' 'It's not a normal equation.' 'The variable is under a radical.' WONDER: 'How do you undo a square root?' 'Do you square both sides?' 'Could there be no solution?' KEY MOVE in Launch: surface that squaring both sides is a one-way operation \u2014 it can create solutions that don't satisfy the original. That's why CHECK is non-negotiable.\",\n 151:       },\n 152:       { id:\"launch\", title:\"Launch \u2014 Example 1 + Example 3 (extraneous solutions)\",\n 153:         fw:\"teacher models\", dok:\"1-2\", min:12,\n 154:         items:[],\n 155:         teacher:[\n 156:           \"Bridge from Do Now: 'You noticed the radical. To undo it, we square both sides \u2014 but that move can create fake solutions, so we always check.'\",\n 157:           \"Project Example 1. Think aloud: isolate the radical, square both sides, solve, check the candidate in the ORIGINAL equation.\",\n 158:           \"Project Example 3. Think aloud: isolate, square, solve, check \u2014 one candidate fails (extraneous). Cross it out.\",\n 159:           \"Pause 1\u00d7 for 30-sec partner-check: 'In Example 1, what step would catch a fake solution?'\",\n 160:           \"Do NOT solve Try-Its. Release promptly at minute 17.\"\n 161:         ],\n 162:         rules:[\n 163:           \"ISOLATE\u2013RAISE\u2013CHECK PROCEDURE:\",\n 164:           \"1. Isolate the radical on one side of the equation.\",\n 165:           \"2. Raise BOTH sides to the index power (square for \u221a, cube for \u221b, etc.).\",\n 166:           \"3. Solve the resulting polynomial equation.\",\n 167:           \"4. CHECK each candidate in the ORIGINAL equation \u2014 reject any that don't satisfy it (extraneous).\",\n 168:           \"\u26a0\ufe0f COMMON ERROR: Do NOT skip the CHECK step \u2014 squaring both sides is not a reversible operation. Always substitute into the ORIGINAL, not the squared form.\"\n 169:         ],\n 170:       },\n 171:       { id:\"explore\", title:\"Explore \u2014 Think-Pair-Share (6 items)\",\n 172:         fw:\"student work\", dok:\"1-2\", min:33,\n 173:         items:[\n 174:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-try-it-1-lesson-5-4\", label:\"Try It 1 \u2014 Isolate and raise to solve\"},\n 175:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-try-it-3-lesson-5-4\", label:\"Try It 3 \u2014 Solve and check for extraneous solutions\"},\n 176:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q22\", label:\"Practice #22 \u2014 Solve \u221a(4x) = 11\"},\n 177:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q8\",  label:\"Practice #8 \u2014 Solve \u221a(8x+9) = x\"},\n 178:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q15\", label:\"Practice #15 \u2014 Identify extraneous solutions (generalize)\"},\n 179:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q4\",  label:\"Practice #4 \u2014 Error Analysis (Nazim, DOK-2 stretch)\"},\n 180:         ],\n 181:         teacher:[\n 182:           \"5 laps across 33 min (\u223c6\u20137 min each).\",\n 183:           \"Lap 1: Try-It 1 \u2014 press pairs to isolate first before squaring.\",\n 184:           \"Lap 2: Try-It 3 \u2014 press pairs to show the check step explicitly.\",\n 185:           \"Lap 3: #22 + #8 \u2014 check that pairs are substituting into the ORIGINAL equation.\",\n 186:           \"Lap 4: #15 \u2014 verify pairs identify and label the extraneous solution.\",\n 187:           \"Lap 5: #4 (extension) \u2014 stretch. Cut if pace slows.\",\n 188:           \"Do not give answers. Sentence frame: 'I isolated the radical to get ___. Squaring gives ___. My candidate solutions are ___. When I check in the ORIGINAL, ___ is valid and ___ is extraneous because ___.' \"\n 189:         ],\n 190:         answer:\"Try It 1: a. x=6; b. x=9. Try It 3: a. x=8 (x=\u22121 is extraneous); b. x=\u22121, x=\u22122. #22: x=30.25 (squaring both sides: 4x=121). #8: candidates 9 and \u22121; x=9 is the valid solution (x=\u22121 is extraneous \u2014 check in original). #15: substitute each candidate into the original equation \u2014 if it makes the equation true, it's a real solution; otherwise extraneous. #4: Nazim didn't check for extraneous solutions \u2014 x=6 is the real solution; x=\u22123 is extraneous.\",\n 191:         warn:\"Period A & F get 65 min for P1. Use the extra 10 min on Explore, NOT Launch. Suggested add: project #15 and #4 for 2 more TPS rounds, or extend Model & Discuss Parts B+C ('write one more radical equation that has an extraneous solution, then solve and reject it').\",\n 192:       },\n 193:       { id:\"share\", title:\"Share / Summary\",\n 194:         fw:\"academic conversation\", dok:\"2\", min:5,\n 195:         items:[],\n 196:         teacher:[\n 197:           \"Cold-call 2 pairs to read their sentence-frame sentence.\",\n 198:           \"Write one student-generated sentence on board.\",\n 199:           \"Preview P2: 'Next class \u2014 rational exponents and a real-world half-life model. Same Isolate\u2013Raise\u2013Check logic applies.'\"\n 200:         ],\n 201:         bridge:\"P2: rational-exponent equations (x^(m/n) form) + DOK-3 Half-Life Model (Practice #41). The Isolate\u2013Raise\u2013Check procedure is identical \u2014 the reciprocal power replaces squaring.\",\n 202:       },\n 203:       { id:\"exit\", title:\"Exit Ticket \u2014 Summary Recap\",\n 204:         fw:\"not graded\", dok:\"1-2\", min:2,\n 205:         items:[],\n 206:         teacher:[\"Collect at door.\",\"Scan fill-ins \u2014 misconceptions about the check step or extraneous solutions tell you what to re-hit in P2 Do Now.\"],\n 207:         rules:[\n 208:           \"To solve a radical equation I first ___.\",\n 209:           \"I raise both sides to power ___ because ___.\",\n 210:           \"An extraneous solution happens when ___.\",\n 211:           \"One biggest thing I learned today: ___\"\n 212:         ],\n 213:       },\n 214:     ]\n 215:   },\n 216: \n 217:   p2: {\n 218:     label: \"Period 2 \u00b7 Rational Exponents + DOK-3 Half-Life Model \u00b7 55 min base\",\n 219:     total_min: 55,\n 220:     short_note: \"P2 IS the DOK-3 spine day. Practice #41 (Half-Life Model) is the single most important item in Topic 5 \u2014 all three assessment items chain off this skill. Pace: Try-It 4 (4 min) \u2192 #12 (4 min) \u2192 #33 (4 min) \u2192 #34 (4 min) \u2192 #41 (12 min). If running tight: cut #33 or #34. NEVER cut #41.\",\n 221:     phases: [\n 222:       { id:\"donow\", title:\"Do Now \u2014 Bridge from P1 (radical isolation)\",\n 223:         fw:\"bridge from P1\", dok:\"1\", min:5,\n 224:         items:[{qid:\"5-4-savvas-q21\", label:\"Practice #21 \u2014 Solve \u221bx + 8 = 13\"}],\n 225:         teacher:[\n 226:           \"Hand out at door.\",\n 227:           \"Circulate 3 min.\",\n 228:           \"Cold-call 1 student for radical isolation and solution check.\"\n 229:         ],\n 230:         rules:[\n 231:           \"Sentence frame: 'I isolate the radical, giving ___. I raise both sides to the power ___, getting ___. I check by substituting back: the left side equals ___ and the right side equals ___. The solution is ___.' \"\n 232:         ],\n 233:         answer:\"x = 125. Isolate: \u221bx = 5. Cube both sides: x = 5\u00b3 = 125. Check: \u221b125 + 8 = 5 + 8 = 13 \u2713\",\n 234:       },\n 235:       { id:\"launch\", title:\"Launch \u2014 Example 4 (rational-exponent equations)\",\n 236:         fw:\"teacher models\", dok:\"2\", min:12,\n 237:         items:[],\n 238:         teacher:[\n 239:           \"Project Example 4. Think aloud: rewrite the rational exponent as a radical, then raise both sides to the reciprocal power.\",\n 240:           \"'The exponent m/n means I raise to the n/m to undo it \u2014 the reciprocal flips the fraction.'\",\n 241:           \"Flag the Rational-Exponent Rules card \u2014 print it in student minds.\",\n 242:           \"30-sec pause: 'What is the reciprocal of the exponent? Can an extraneous solution appear?' (partner check)\",\n 243:           \"Today's DOK-3 task applies this exact skill to a real-world decay model: y = a\u00b7(0.5)^(t/h).\",\n 244:           \"Do NOT solve Try-Its or Practice items \u2014 release at minute 17.\"\n 245:         ],\n 246:         rules:[\n 247:           \"RATIONAL-EXPONENT RULES:\",\n 248:           \"1. Rewrite x^(m/n) as (\u207fx)\u1d50 \u2014 bridge to radicals you already know.\",\n 249:           \"2. To solve (expr)^(m/n) = k, raise both sides to the reciprocal power n/m.\",\n 250:           \"3. Simplify, then CHECK \u2014 extraneous solutions arise when the denominator n is EVEN.\",\n 251:           \"4. For real-world decay y = a\u00b7(0.5)^(t/h): half-life h tells you the exponent's denominator; plug in t, evaluate.\",\n 252:           \"\u26a0\ufe0f EXTRANEOUS: if n (denominator) is EVEN, raising to even power can introduce false solutions. ALWAYS substitute back into the ORIGINAL.\"\n 253:         ],\n 254:       },\n 255:       { id:\"explore\", title:\"Explore \u2014 TPS + \u2b50 DOK-3 Half-Life Model\",\n 256:         fw:\"student work + DOK-3\", dok:\"2-3\", min:33,\n 257:         items:[\n 258:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-try-it-4-lesson-5-4\", label:\"Try It 4 \u2014 Rational-exponent equations (a. & b.)\"},\n 259:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q12\", label:\"Practice #12 \u2014 (3x+2)^(2/3) = 4\"},\n 260:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q33\", label:\"Practice #33 \u2014 0.5(x\u00b2+5x+136)^(2/3) = 50\"},\n 261:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q34\", label:\"Practice #34 \u2014 2(x\u00b2\u221212x\u22124)^(1/2) \u2212 3 = 15\"},\n 262:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q41\", label:\"Practice #41 \u2b50 DOK-3 HALF-LIFE MODEL \u00b7 Soft-drink half-life (5 h, 50 mL, t=16 h)\", dok3:true, note:\"Identify a=50, h=5, t=16 \u2192 compute 16/5=3.2 \u2192 evaluate 50\u00b7(0.5)^3.2 \u2248 5.44 mL \u2192 interpret. Plan ~12 min.\"},\n 263:         ],\n 264:         teacher:[\n 265:           \"4 laps across 33 min.\",\n 266:           \"Lap 1 (8 min): Try-It 4 + #12 \u2014 press pairs to write the reciprocal power EXPLICITLY.\",\n 267:           \"Lap 2 (8 min): #33 + #34 \u2014 watch for pairs who skip the check step.\",\n 268:           \"Lap 3 (12 min): \u2b50 #41 Half-Life Model. Students identify a, h, t; compute exponent; evaluate; interpret.\",\n 269:           \"BEFORE #41 say: 'Two prior versions \u2014 Venetta was polynomial, chemist was rational. Today is exponential decay: y = 50\u00b7(0.5)^(t/5). Same question every time: for what input does the machine give this output?'\",\n 270:           \"Optional: after solving, ask 'What's the half-life if the function were y = 50\u00b7(0.5)^(t/8)?' \u2014 forces students to notice the parameter controls the machine's behavior.\",\n 271:           \"Do NOT give #41 answer. Pairs present computation and interpretation at Share.\"\n 272:         ],\n 273:         answer:\"Try It 4: x=10, x=\u22127. #12: x=2 or x=\u221210/3 (from (3x+2)^(2/3)=4 \u2192 3x+2=\u00b18 \u2192 x=2 or x=\u221210/3). #33: x=27 and x=\u221232. #34: x=\u22125 and x=17. \u2b50 #41: y = 50\u00b7(0.5)^(16/5) = 50\u00b7(0.5)^3.2 \u2248 5.44 mL. Interpret: 'About 5.44 mL of the drink remains after 16 hours.' All three Topic 5 assessment items chain off this skill.\",\n 274:         warn:\"65-min classes: use extra 10 min on Explore #41 \u2014 press students to write the FULL interpretation sentence, not just the decimal. If finished early: Practice #19 (extraneous-solution argument) as fast-finisher.\",\n 275:       },\n 276:       { id:\"share\", title:\"Share / Summary + P3 Bridge\",\n 277:         fw:\"academic conversation\", dok:\"2\", min:5,\n 278:         items:[],\n 279:         teacher:[\n 280:           \"Cold-call 1 pair (one that struggled on #41) to present computation and interpretation using sentence frame.\",\n 281:           \"Class signals thumbs. Write the half-life formula y = a\u00b7(0.5)^(t/h) on board with a, h, t labeled.\",\n 282:           \"P3 bridge: 'Next period we apply these skills to assessment-critical items \u2014 bring your check-for-extraneous habit.'\"\n 283:         ],\n 284:         rules:[\n 285:           \"Sentence frame (Half-Life Model): 'This equation has exponent ___/___, so I raise both sides to the power ___/___. After simplifying I get ___. In the half-life formula, a = ___ (start), h = ___ (half-life period), t = ___ (elapsed time), giving y \u2248 ___.' \",\n 286:           \"Whole class: one pair shares computation and interpretation. Class signals thumbs up/sideways.\"\n 287:         ],\n 288:         answer:\"Exponent t/h represents the number of half-life cycles elapsed. Does a rational exponent always produce a unique solution? No \u2014 check for extraneous when denominator is even.\",\n 289:         bridge:\"P3: rewrite radical/rational-exponent formulas to isolate a specified variable. Assessment-critical: Topic 5 Performance Items 1A/1B (pyramid-volume formula \u2192 solve for x \u2192 evaluate) and Item 2 (complete the table). Practice #10 = Item 1A rehearsal; Practice #44 = Item 2 rehearsal.\",\n 290:       },\n 291:       { id:\"exit\", title:\"Exit Ticket \u2014 Summary Recap\",\n 292:         fw:\"not graded\", dok:\"1-2\", min:3,\n 293:         items:[],\n 294:         teacher:[\"Collect at door.\",\"Scan stem 3 (50 \u2192 5.44 mL). If many students got the computation wrong or skipped the interpretation, plan a 5-min P3 Do Now recap.\"],\n 295:         rules:[\n 296:           \"To solve (expr)^(m/n) = k I raise both sides to power ___.\",\n 297:           \"Half-life tells me the ___ of the exponent.\",\n 298:           \"After 16 hours, 50 mL of soft drink decays to ___ mL.\",\n 299:           \"One biggest thing I learned today: ___\"\n 300:         ],\n 301:       },\n 302:     ]\n 303:   },\n 304: \n 305:   p3: {\n 306:     label: \"Period 3 \u00b7 Solve for Variable + Modeling \u00b7 55 min base \u00b7 ASSESSMENT-CRITICAL\",\n 307:     total_min: 55,\n 308:     short_note: \"P3 prepares students for Topic 5 Performance Assessment: Item 1A (rewrite pyramid-volume formula for x), Item 1B (evaluate), and Item 2 (complete the table). Practice #10 IS Item 1A rehearsal. Practice #44 (done in Launch) IS Item 2 rehearsal. No DOK-3 driver \u2014 P2 owned the spine. Pure DOK-2 mastery day.\",\n 309:     phases: [\n 310:       { id:\"donow\", title:\"Do Now \u2014 Bridge from P2 (extraneous HOT \u2014 DOK-3)\",\n 311:         fw:\"bridge from P2\", dok:\"3\", min:5,\n 312:         items:[{qid:\"5-4-savvas-q19\", label:\"Practice #19 \u2014 HOT: when do rational-exponent equations have extraneous solutions?\"}],\n 313:         teacher:[\n 314:           \"Hand out at door.\",\n 315:           \"Circulate 3 min.\",\n 316:           \"Cold-call for extraneous-solution reasoning.\",\n 317:           \"30-sec prediction before Launch: 'If you solved V = (1/3)\u03c0r\u00b2h for r, would you expect an extraneous solution? Why or why not?'\"\n 318:         ],\n 319:         answer:\"Extraneous solutions arise when a solving step raises both sides to an EVEN power (or undoes an even root), because that erases sign information. Odd powers and odd roots preserve sign \u2014 no false-solution risk. Any candidate should still be checked. Prediction for V formula: r\u00b2 involved \u2192 possible extraneous, but physical constraint r > 0 eliminates negatives.\",\n 320:       },\n 321:       { id:\"launch\", title:\"Launch \u2014 Example 2 (rewrite formula) + Practice #44 (table completion)\",\n 322:         fw:\"teacher models 2 examples\", dok:\"2\", min:12,\n 323:         items:[],\n 324:         teacher:[\n 325:           \"FIRST 6 min \u2014 Example 2 (Golden Gate cables): identify target variable. Undo operations LAST-IN FIRST-OUT (subtract, divide, raise to reciprocal power). Emphasize: (m/n) \u2192 (n/m) flips the exponent.\",\n 326:           \"NEXT 5 min \u2014 Practice #44 (table-completion): show rewritten formula first, then substitute each row's known values. Demonstrate calculator for irrational outputs.\",\n 327:           \"Say: 'This IS Assessment Item 2 \u2014 same structure, same workflow. Any student who can complete #44 can complete Item 2.'\",\n 328:           \"30 sec: point at rule cards. 'Mark them. These are your assessment reference.'\",\n 329:           \"Do NOT solve Try-It 2 or Explore items. Release at minute 17.\"\n 330:         ],\n 331:         rules:[\n 332:           \"ISOLATE-A-VARIABLE RULES:\",\n 333:           \"1. Identify which variable to isolate (look at the final answer form).\",\n 334:           \"2. Strip away outer operations LAST-IN, FIRST-OUT (add/sub \u2192 multiply/divide \u2192 exponent/radical).\",\n 335:           \"3. Raise both sides to the RECIPROCAL power to undo a rational exponent (m/n \u2192 n/m).\",\n 336:           \"TABLE-EVALUATION RULES:\",\n 337:           \"1. For each input value, substitute into the rewritten formula.\",\n 338:           \"2. Simplify rational exponents step-by-step (use calculator for irrational outputs).\",\n 339:           \"3. Round to specified precision.\",\n 340:           \"4. The table is a point-cloud of the formula's graph \u2014 plot the points to see the shape.\"\n 341:         ],\n 342:         answer:\"Example 2 (Golden Gate): y \u2248 0.000112x\u00b2 + 220; at x=200, y \u2248 224.48 ft above roadway. Practice #44 (y = \u221b(2x+z) \u2212 12): completed table \u2014 row 1 (y=0, x=462): z=804; row 2 (y=\u22123, z=439): x=145; row 3 (y=\u221210, x=1.25): z=5.5; row 4 (y=3, z=16): x=1679.5.\",\n 343:         warn:\"\u2b50 Practice #44 is the algebraic twin of Topic 5 Assessment Item 2. Walk through at least the first two rows live so students see the calculator workflow for irrational outputs.\",\n 344:       },\n 345:       { id:\"explore\", title:\"Explore \u2014 TPS \u00b7 Pure DOK-2 Mastery \u00b7 Assessment Rehearsal (6 items)\",\n 346:         fw:\"student work \u00b7 pure DOK-2\", dok:\"2\", min:35,\n 347:         items:[\n 348:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-try-it-2-lesson-5-4\", label:\"Try It 2 \u2014 Rewrite formula: stopping distance (v = 3.57\u221ad)\"},\n 349:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q10\", label:\"Practice #10 \u2b50 ASSESSMENT ITEM 1A \u2014 Isolate x from y = \u221a((x\u221248)/6)\", assess:true, note:\"Direct rehearsal of Topic 5 Assessment Item 1A. Plan ~7 min. After solving, ask: 'Could you now complete a table using this formula?'\"},\n 350:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q25\", label:\"Practice #25 \u2014 Solve for y: x = 3\u221b(15+y)\"},\n 351:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q26\", label:\"Practice #26 \u2014 Solve for y: x = \u221a(2y)/26\"},\n 352:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q27\", label:\"Practice #27 \u2014 Solve for y: x = \u221a(y\u221214.2)/0.05\"},\n 353:           {qid:\"5-4-savvas-q39\", label:\"Practice #39 \u2014 BSA Application (H=165 cm, BSA < 2.0, find max mass)\"},\n 354:         ],\n 355:         teacher:[\n 356:           \"6 laps across 35 min.\",\n 357:           \"Laps 1\u20132: Try-It 2 + #10 \u2014 isolate-a-variable. Press reciprocal-power step on every pair.\",\n 358:           \"Laps 3\u20135: #25 + #26 + #27 \u2014 solve for y, rationalization pattern set. Ask 'what's the same? what changes?'\",\n 359:           \"Lap 6: #39 \u2014 BSA application. Press interpretation: what do the units tell you about whether the answer is reasonable?\",\n 360:           \"CRITICAL: #10 IS Assessment Item 1A. After #10, ask 'Could you now complete a table using your answer? Try one row.'\",\n 361:           \"If pace slows: cut #27 first. NEVER cut #10.\"\n 362:         ],\n 363:         answer:\"Try It 2: d = (v/3.57)\u00b2. #10: x = 6y\u00b2 + 48 (THIS IS Assessment Item 1A \u2014 rewrite y=\u221a((x\u221248)/6) for x). #25: y = x\u00b3/27 \u2212 15. #26: y = 338x\u00b2. #27: y = 0.0025x\u00b2 + 14.2. #39: BSA = \u221a(H\u00b7M/3600) with H=165 \u2192 solve for M: M < 87.3 kg.\",\n 364:         warn:\"\u2b50 Practice #10 IS Topic 5 Assessment Item 1A. Assessment Items 1A/1B: rewrite pyramid-volume formula for x, then evaluate. Item 2: complete a table. Students who own #10 + #44 own at least 2 of the 3 performance assessment items.\",\n 365:       },\n 366:       { id:\"share\", title:\"Share / Summary \u2014 Concept Summary + Assessment Preview\",\n 367:         fw:\"assessment preview\", dok:\"2\", min:5,\n 368:         items:[],\n 369:         teacher:[\n 370:           \"Cold-call 1 pair to present #10 (isolate x) using the sentence frame.\",\n 371:           \"Project Concept Summary from student packet.\",\n 372:           \"'Topic 5 Performance Assessment: Items 1A/1B ask you to rewrite a pyramid-volume formula for x, then evaluate. Item 2 asks you to complete a table. You just rehearsed BOTH.'\"\n 373:         ],\n 374:         rules:[\n 375:           \"CONCEPT SUMMARY \u2014 ISOLATING A VARIABLE:\",\n 376:           \"Identify the variable to isolate \u2192 undo outer ops LAST-IN FIRST-OUT \u2192 raise to reciprocal power \u2192 simplify.\",\n 377:           \"To undo x^(m/n): raise both sides to (n/m).\",\n 378:           \"COMPLETING A TABLE: use rewritten formula as the rule for each row; substitute V-value; calculator for irrational outputs; round as directed.\",\n 379:           \"Assessment Item 2 expects THIS process \u2014 rewritten formula \u2192 table.\"\n 380:         ],\n 381:         bridge:\"Topic 5 Performance Assessment rehearsal complete: Item 1A = isolate x (#10 done \u2714), Item 2 = complete the table (#44 done in Launch \u2714). You own 2 of 3 performance assessment items.\",\n 382:       },\n 383:       { id:\"exit\", title:\"Exit Ticket \u2014 Pre-Assessment Confidence Check\",\n 384:         fw:\"confidence check\", dok:\"1-2\", min:3,\n 385:         items:[],\n 386:         teacher:[\"Collect at door.\",\"Tonight: scan for students who can't complete the reciprocal-power step or the table row. Insert a 5-min Do Now recap when the Performance Assessment lands.\"],\n 387:         rules:[\n 388:           \"To isolate a variable from a rational-exponent formula I ___.\",\n 389:           \"When I evaluate the formula at a specific x, I ___.\",\n 390:           \"One biggest thing I learned today: ___\"\n 391:         ],\n 392:       },\n 393:     ]\n 394:   },\n 395: };\n 396: \n 397: const panels = document.getElementById(\"panels\");\n 398: const tabBar = document.getElementById(\"tabBar\");\n 399: document.getElementById(\"dateLabel\").textContent = new Date().toDateString();\n 400: \n 401: function renderPanel(tabId) {\n 402:   const data = PACER[tabId];\n 403:   const panel = document.createElement(\"div\");\n 404:   panel.className = \"panel\";\n 405:   panel.id = \"panel-\" + tabId;\n 406:   panel.innerHTML = `\n 407:     <div class=\"timer-bar\">\n 408:       <div>\n 409:         <div class=\"phase-title\" id=\"phaseTitle-${tabId}\">Select a phase to begin</div>\n 410:       </div>\n 411:       <div class=\"countdown\" id=\"timer-${tabId}\">--:--</div>\n 412:       <div>\n 413:         <button onclick=\"startPhase('${tabId}')\" id=\"startBtn-${tabId}\">Start</button>\n 414:         <button class=\"stop\" onclick=\"stopPhase('${tabId}')\">Stop</button>\n 415:       </div>\n 416:     </div>\n 417:     <div style=\"font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 8px;\">${data.label}</div>\n 418:     ${data.short_note ? `<div style=\"background:#fffbea; border-left:4px solid var(--gold); padding:10px 14px; border-radius:6px; font-size:0.9rem; margin:12px 0;\">&#11088; ${data.short_note}</div>` : \"\"}\n 419:     <div class=\"phase-list\" id=\"chips-${tabId}\"></div>\n 420:     <div id=\"phase-card-${tabId}\"></div>\n 421:   `;\n 422:   panels.appendChild(panel);\n 423:   const chipBar = panel.querySelector(`#chips-${tabId}`);\n 424:   data.phases.forEach((ph, idx) => {\n 425:     const chip = document.createElement(\"button\");\n 426:     chip.className = \"phase-chip\";\n 427:     chip.textContent = `${idx+1}. ${ph.title.split(\" \u2014 \")[0]} \u00b7 ${ph.min}m`;\n 428:     chip.onclick = () => selectPhase(tabId, idx);\n 429:     chipBar.appendChild(chip);\n 430:   });\n 431:   selectPhase(tabId, 0);\n 432: }\n 433: \n 434: function selectPhase(tabId, idx) {\n 435:   const data = PACER[tabId];\n 436:   const ph = data.phases[idx];\n 437:   const card = document.getElementById(`phase-card-${tabId}`);\n 438:   let html = `<div class=\"phase-card\">\n 439:     <h2>${ph.title}\n 440:       <span class=\"ba