el & Discuss (lesson 5-4 Launch)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"EXPLORE & REASON\\n\\nA. Solve (3(a+1)^2+2=11). Use at least two different methods.\\n\\nB. Try each of the methods you used in part A to solve (3\u221a(a+1)+2=11).\\n\\nC. Generalize Which of the methods is better suited for solving an equation with a radical? What problems arise when using the other method?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 3, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 3 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"launch\", \"model-discuss\", \"savvas-declared-launch\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): (a=-1\u00b1\u221a(3)); You can solve this by isolating ((a+1)^2) and then taking the square root of each side. Or you could graph each side of the equation on the same coordinate grid and find the intersection points.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 351: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-1-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 1 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve an Equation With One Radical\\n\\nA. Solve the radical equation \u221a(x + 5) - 1 = 3.\\n\\nTo solve this equation, you can isolate the radical. Then you can square both sides of the equation to eliminate the radical and solve for x.\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x + 5) - 1 &= 3 \\n\u221a(x + 5) &= 4 \\n(\u221a(x + 5))^2 &= 4^2 \\nx + 5 &= 16 \\nx &= 11\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nSo the solution to the radical equation is x = 11.\\n\\nYou can check by graphing y = \u221a(x + 5) - 1 and y = 3 on the same coordinate axes. The graphs of the equations intersect at (11, 3).\\n\\n\\\\begin{center}\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\\\end{center}\\n\\n\\\\textbf{B. Solve the radical equation \\\\sqrt[3]{x} + 2 = 4.}\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\\\\sqrt[3]{x} + 2 &= 4 \\n\\\\sqrt[3]{x} &= 2 \\n(\\\\sqrt[3]{x})^3 &= 2^3 \\nx &= 8\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nSo the solution to the radical equation is x = 8.\\n\\nCheck your answer by substituting 8 for x in the original equation:\\n\\\\sqrt[3]{8} + 2 = 2 + 2 = 4\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-1\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Solve an Equation With One Radical  A. Solve the radical equation \u221a(x + 5) - 1 =' \u2192 [solve-radical-equation, isolate-radical, solve-for-variable]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\", \"isolate-radical\", \"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 352: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-2-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 2 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Rewrite a Formula\\n\\nThe suspension cables from the Golden Gate Bridge's towers are farther above the roadway near the towers and closer to the roadway near the middle of the bridge. You can figure out your distance from the middle of the bridge, x, in feet, and the height of the suspension cable, y, in feet, by using the given equation. About how far is the cable from the roadway when you are 200 ft from the middle of the bridge?\\n\\n\\\\placeholder{photo}{Golden Gate Bridge with the equation x = \\\\frac{\u221a(y - 220)}{0.010583} pointing to the suspension cable.}\\n\\nRewrite the equation to isolate y so you can express the vertical distance between the roadway of the bridge and the suspension cable in terms of x.\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\nx &= \\\\frac{\u221a(y - 220)}{0.010583} \\n0.010583x &= \u221a(y - 220) \\n(0.010583x)^2 &= (\u221a(y - 220))^2 \\n0.000112x^2 &\u2248 y - 220 \\n0.000112x^2 + 220 &\u2248 y \\n0.000112(200)^2 + 220 &\u2248 y \\n224.48 &\u2248 y\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nThe cable is about 224 ft above the bridge's roadway when you are 200 ft from the middle of the bridge.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-2\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"P3 Launch. Golden Gate Bridge cables \u2014 rewrite formula.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"launch-model-1\", \"standards\": [\"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"5-4-savvas-example-4-lesson-5-4\"], \"rehearses\": [\"5-4-savvas-try-it-2-lesson-5-4\", \"5-4-savvas-q10\"], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"rewrite-formula\", \"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 353: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-3-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 3 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Identify an Extraneous Solution\\n\\nA. Solve the radical equation \u221a(3x - 2) = x - 4.\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(3x - 2) &= x - 4 \\n(\u221a(3x - 2))^2 &= (x - 4)^2 \\n3x - 2 &= x^2 - 8x + 16 \\n0 &= x^2 - 11x + 18 \\n0 &= (x - 9)(x - 2) \\nx = 9 &or x = 2\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nCheck the potential solutions by substituting the solutions for x in the original equation:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(3x - 2) &= x - 4 & \u221a(3x - 2) &= x - 4 \\n\u221a(3(9) - 2) &= 9 - 4 & \u221a(3(2) - 2) &= 2 - 4 \\n\u221a(25) &= 5 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}} & \u221a(4) &\u2260 -2 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\ding{55}}}\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nSo 9 is the only solution to the equation and 2 is an extraneous solution.\\n\\nB. Why does this extraneous solution arise?\\n\\nThe first step in solving the equation in part (a) was to square both sides. \\n\\nGraph on one grid, y = \u221a(3x - 2) and y = x - 4. Then graph on another grid y = 3x - 2 and y = (x - 4)^2.\\n\\n\\\\begin{center}\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n \\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\\\end{center}\\n\\nThis means that the equations \u221a(3x - 2) = x - 4 and (\u221a(3x - 2))^2 = (x - 4)^2 are not equivalent equations. Both graphs have an intersection point at x = 9, but the second graph also has an intersection point at x = 2. Squaring both sides of the original equation created an extraneous solution.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-3\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Identify an Extraneous Solution  A. Solve the radical equation \u221a(3x - 2) = x - 4' \u2192 [graph-intersection, identify-extraneous-solution, solve-radical-equation]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"graph-intersection\", \"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 354: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-4-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 4 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve Equations With Rational Exponents\\n\\n\\\\textbf{A. What are the solutions to the equation (x^2 + 5x + 5)^{5/2} = 1?}\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(x^2 + 5x + 5)^{5/2} &= 1 \\n((x^2 + 5x + 5)^{5/2})^{2/5} &= (1)^{2/5} \\nx^2 + 5x + 5 &= 1 \\nx^2 + 5x + 4 &= 0 \\n(x + 4)(x + 1) &= 0 \\nx + 4 = 0 &or x + 1 = 0 \\nx = -4 &or x = -1\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nCheck for extraneous solutions.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n((-4)^2 + 5(-4) + 5)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 & ((-1)^2 + 5(-1) + 5)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 \\n(16 - 20 + 5)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 & (1 - 5 + 5)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 \\n(1)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 & (1)^{5/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 1 \\n1 &= 1 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}} & 1 &= 1 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}}\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nThis equation has two solutions, x = -4 and x = -1. There are no extraneous solutions.\\n\\n\\\\textbf{B. What is the solution to (x + 18)^{3/2} = (x - 2)^3?}\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(x + 18)^{3/2} &= (x - 2)^3 \\n((x + 18)^{3/2})^{2/3} &= ((x - 2)^3)^{2/3} \\nx + 18 &= (x - 2)^2 \\nx + 18 &= x^2 - 4x + 4 \\nx^2 - 5x - 14 &= 0 \\n(x + 2)(x - 7) &= 0 \\nx + 2 = 0 &or x - 7 = 0 \\nx = -2 &or x = 7\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nCheck for extraneous solutions.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(-2 + 18)^{3/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} (-2 - 2)^3 & (7 + 18)^{3/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} (7 - 2)^3 \\n16^{3/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} (-4)^3 & 25^{3/2} &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 5^3 \\n64 &\u2260 -64 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\ding{55}}} & 125 &= 125 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}}\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nSo 7 is the only solution to the equation, and -2 is an extraneous solution.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-4\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"P2 Launch. Rational-exponent equations \u2014 bridge from 5-1 rational-exp evaluation to 5-4 equation-solving.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"launch-model-1\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"5-1-savvas-example-3-lesson-5-1-2\", \"5-4-savvas-try-it-3-lesson-5-4\"], \"rehearses\": [\"5-4-savvas-try-it-4-lesson-5-4\", \"5-4-savvas-q12\"], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-rational-exponent-equation\", \"raise-to-reciprocal-power\"]}\n 355: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-5-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 5 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve an Equation With Two Radicals\\n\\nSolve the radical equation \u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x) = 3.\\n\\nWhen an equation has two radicals, start the solution process by isolating one radical.\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x) &= 3 \\n\u221a(x + 9) &= \u221a(2x) + 3 \\n(\u221a(x + 9))^2 &= (\u221a(2x) + 3)^2 \\nx + 9 &= 2x + 6\u221a(2x) + 9 \\n0 &= x + 6\u221a(2x) \\n-x &= 6\u221a(2x) \\n(-x)^2 &= (6\u221a(2x))^2 \\nx^2 - 72x &= 0 \\nx(x - 72) &= 0 \\nx = 0 &or x = 72\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nCheck the potential solutions by substituting 0 and 72 for x in the original equation:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 & \u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 \\n\u221a((0) + 9) - \u221a(2(0)) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 & \u221a((72) + 9) - \u221a(2(72)) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 \\n\u221a(9) - \u221a(0) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 & \u221a(81) - \u221a(144) &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 \\n3 - 0 &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 & 9 - 12 &\\\\stackrel{?}{=} 3 \\n3 &= 3 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}} & -3 &\u2260 3 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\ding{55}}}\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nThe only solution is 0. The value 72 does not make the original equation true, so it is an extraneous solution.\\n\\nYou can also see this by graphing y = \u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x) and y = 3 together. They intersect only at x = 0.\\n\\n\\\\begin{center}\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\\\end{center}\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-5\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Solve an Equation With Two Radicals  Solve the radical equation \u221a(x + 9) - \u221a(2x)' \u2192 [identify-extraneous-solution, solve-radical-equation]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 356: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-6-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 6 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve a Radical Inequality\\n\\nThe body surface area (BSA) of a human being is used to determine some doses of medication. The formula for finding BSA is BSA = \u221a((H \u00b7 M)/(3,600)), where H is the height in centimeters and M is the mass in kilograms.\\n\\nA doctor calculates a particular dose of medicine for a patient. What must the mass of the person be for the dose to be appropriate?\\n\\n\\\\placeholder{illustration}{A doctor looking at a tablet. The tablet shows: BSA < 1.9, Height = 160cm.}\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\nBSA &< 1.9 \\n\u221a((H \u00b7 M)/(3,600)) &< 1.9 \\n\u221a((160 \u00b7 M)/(3,600)) &< 1.9 \\n(\u221a((160 \u00b7 M)/(3,600)))^2 &< 1.9^2 \\n(160 \u00b7 M)/(3,600) &< 3.61 \\nM &< 81.225\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nThe mass of the patient must be less than 81.225 kg for the dose to be appropriate.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-6\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Solve a Radical Inequality  The body surface area (BSA) of a human being is used' \u2192 [solve-radical-equation]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 357: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-1-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 1 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve an Equation With One Radical\\n\\nA. Solve the radical equation (\u221a(x+5)-1=3).\\n\\nTo solve this equation, you can isolate the radical. Then you can square both sides of the equation to eliminate the radical and solve for (x).\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x+5)-1&=3\\\\\\n\u221a(x+5)&=4\\\\\\n(\u221a(x+5))^2&=4^2 &&Square both sides to eliminate the radical.\\\\\\nx+5&=16\\\\\\nx&=11\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nSo the solution to the radical equation is (x=11).\\n\\nYou can check by graphing (y=\u221a(x+5)-1) and (y=3) on the same coordinate axes. The graphs of the equations intersect at ((11,3)).\\n\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\nB. Solve the radical equation (\\\\sqrt[3]{x}+2=4).\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\\\\sqrt[3]{x}+2&=4\\\\\\n\\\\sqrt[3]{x}&=2\\\\\\n(\\\\sqrt[3]{x})^3&=2^3 &&Cube both sides to eliminate the cube root.\\\\\\nx&=8\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nSo the solution to the radical equation is (x=8).\\n\\nCheck your answer by substituting 8 for (x) in the original equation:\\n[\\n\\\\sqrt[3]{8}+2=2+2=4.\\n]\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-1\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): A. (x=11); B. (x=8)\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 358: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-2-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 2 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Rewrite a Formula\\n\\nThe suspension cables from the Golden Gate Bridge's towers are farther above the roadway near the towers and closer to the roadway near the middle of the bridge. You can figure out your distance from the middle of the bridge, (x), in feet, and the height of the suspension cable, (y), in feet, by using the given equation. About how far is the cable from the roadway when you are 200 ft from the middle of the bridge?\\n\\n\\\\placeholder{photo}{Golden Gate Bridge suspension cables with formula label (x=\\\\frac{\u221a(y-220)}{0.010583}) pointing to the suspension cable.}\\n\\nRewrite the equation to isolate (y) so you can express the vertical distance between the roadway of the bridge and the suspension cable in terms of (x).\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\nx&=\\\\frac{\u221a(y-220)}{0.010583}\\\\\\n0.010583x&=\u221a(y-220)\\\\\\n(0.010583x)^2&=(\u221a(y-220))^2\\\\\\n0.000112x^2&\u2248 y-220\\\\\\n0.000112x^2+220&\u2248 y\\\\\\n0.000112(200)^2+220&\u2248 y\\\\\\n224.48&\u2248 y\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nThis equation gives the vertical distance between the deck and the suspension cable for different values of (x).\\n\\nThe cable is about 224 ft above the bridge's roadway when you are 200 ft from the middle of the bridge.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-2\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): The cable is about 224 ft above the bridge's roadway.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 359: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-3-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 3 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Identify an Extraneous Solution\\n\\nA. Solve the radical equation (\u221a(3x-2)=x-4).\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(3x-2)&=x-4\\\\\\n(\u221a(3x-2))^2&=(x-4)^2\\\\\\n3x-2&=x^2-8x+16\\\\\\n0&=x^2-11x+18\\\\\\n0&=(x-9)(x-2)\\\\\\nx&=9 or x=2\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nCheck the potential solutions by substituting the solutions for (x) in the original equation:\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(3x-2)&=x-4 & \u221a(3x-2)&=x-4\\\\\\n\u221a(3(9)-2)&=9-4 & \u221a(3(2)-2)&=2-4\\\\\\n\u221a(25)&=5 & \u221a(4)&\\\\ne -2\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nSo 9 is the only solution to the equation and 2 is an extraneous solution.\\n\\nB. Why does this extraneous solution arise?\\n\\nThe first step in solving the equation in part (a) was to square both sides.\\n\\nGraph on one grid, (y=\u221a(3x-2)) and (y=x-4). Then graph on another grid (y=3x-2) and (y=(x-4)^2).\\n\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n \\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\nThese intersect at ((9,5)), so the solution to (\u221a(3x-2)=x-4) is (x=9).\\n\\nThese intersect at ((2,4)) and ((9,25)), so the solutions to ((\u221a(3x-2))^2=(x-4)^2) are (x=2) and (x=9).\\n\\nThis means that the equations (\u221a(3x-2)=x-4) and ((\u221a(3x-2))^2=(x-4)^2) are not equivalent equations. Both graphs have an intersection point at (x=9), but the second graph also has an intersection point at (x=2). Squaring both sides of the original equation created an extraneous solution.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-3\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): (x=9); (x=2) is an extraneous solution.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 360: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-4-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 4 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve Equations With Rational Exponents\\n\\nA. What are the solutions to the equation ((x^2+5x+5)^{(3)/(2)}=1)?\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(x^2+5x+5)^{(3)/(2)}&=1\\\\\\n((x^2+5x+5)^{(3)/(2)})^{(2)/(3)}&=(1)^{(2)/(3)} &&Raise both sides to the reciprocal power.\\\\\\nx^2+5x+5&=1\\\\\\nx^2+5x+4&=0\\\\\\n(x+4)(x+1)&=0 &&Use the Zero-Product Property.\\\\\\nx+4&=0 or x+1=0\\\\\\nx&=-4 or x=-1\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nCheck for extraneous solutions.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n((-4)^2+5(-4)+5)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1 & ((-1)^2+5(-1)+5)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1\\\\\\n(16-20+5)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1 & (1-5+5)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1\\\\\\n(1)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1 & (1)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}1\\\\\\n1&=1 & 1&=1\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nThis equation has two solutions, (x=-4) and (x=-1). There are no extraneous solutions.\\n\\nB. What is the solution to ((x+18)^{(3)/(2)}=(x-2)^3)?\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(x+18)^{(3)/(2)}&=(x-2)^3\\\\\\n((x+18)^{(3)/(2)})^{(2)/(3)}&=((x-2)^3)^{(2)/(3)} &&Raise both sides to the reciprocal power.\\\\\\nx+18&=(x-2)^2\\\\\\nx+18&=x^2-4x+4\\\\\\nx^2-5x-14&=0\\\\\\n(x+2)(x-7)&=0\\\\\\nx+2&=0 or x-7=0 &&Use the Zero-Product Property.\\\\\\nx&=-2 or x=7\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nCheck for extraneous solutions.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(-2+18)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}(-2-2)^3 & (7+18)^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}(7-2)^3\\\\\\n16^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}(-4)^3 & 25^{(3)/(2)}&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}5^3\\\\\\n64&\\\\ne -64 & 125&=125\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nSo 7 is the only solution to the equation, and -2 is an extraneous solution.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-4\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): A. (x=-4) and (x=-1); B. (x=7), and (x=-2) is an extraneous solution.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 361: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-5-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 5 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve an Equation With Two Radicals\\n\\nSolve the radical equation (\u221a(x+9)-\u221a(2x)=3).\\n\\nWhen an equation has two radicals, start the solution process by isolating one radical.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x+9)-\u221a(2x)&=3\\\\\\n\u221a(x+9)&=\u221a(2x)+3 &&Isolate one radical sign.\\\\\\n(\u221a(x+9))^2&=(\u221a(2x)+3)^2\\\\\\nx+9&=2x+6\u221a(2x)+9\\\\\\n0&=x+6\u221a(2x)\\\\\\n-x&=6\u221a(2x) &&Isolate the remaining radical and square again.\\\\\\n(-x)^2&=(6\u221a(2x))^2\\\\\\nx^2-72x&=0\\\\\\nx(x-72)&=0\\\\\\nx&=0 or x=72\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nCheck the potential solutions by substituting 0 and 72 for (x) in the original equation:\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n\u221a(x+9)-\u221a(2x)&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3 & \u221a(x+9)-\u221a(2x)&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3\\\\\\n\u221a((0)+9)-\u221a(2(0))&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3 & \u221a((72)+9)-\u221a(2(72))&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3\\\\\\n\u221a(9)-\u221a(0)&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3 & \u221a(81)-\u221a(144)&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3\\\\\\n3-0&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3 & 9-12&\\\\stackrel{?}{=}3\\\\\\n3&=3 & -3&\\\\ne 3\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nThe only solution is 0. The value 72 does not make the original equation true, so it is an extraneous solution.\\n\\nYou can also see this by graphing (y=\u221a(x+9)-\u221a(2x)) and (y=3) together. They intersect only at (x=0).\\n\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-5\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): (x=0); (x=72) is an extraneous solution.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 362: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-example-6-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 6 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solve a Radical Inequality\\n\\nThe body surface area (BSA) of a human being is used to determine some doses of medication. The formula for finding BSA is (BSA=\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{H\u00b7 M}{3{,}600}}), where (H) is the height in centimeters and (M) is the mass in kilograms.\\n\\nA doctor calculates a particular dose of medicine for a patient. What must the mass of the person be for the dose to be appropriate?\\n\\n[IMAGE: Doctor using a tablet while speaking with a patient; tablet text: \\\"BSA < 1.9\\\" and \\\"Height = 160 cm\\\".]\\n\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\nBSA&<1.9\\\\\\n\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{H\u00b7 M}{3{,}600}}&<1.9 &&Substitute in an expression equal to BSA.\\\\\\n\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{160\u00b7 M}{3{,}600}}&<1.9 &&Substitute 160 for (H), and solve for (M).\\\\\\n(\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{160M}{3{,}600}})^2&<1.9^2\\\\\\n\\\\frac{160M}{3{,}600}&<3.61\\\\\\nM&<81.225\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nThe mass of the patient must be less than 81.225 kg for the dose to be appropriate.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-6\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): The mass of the patient must be less than 81.225 kg. \u00b7 IMAGE PLACEHOLDER [illustration]: Doctor using a tablet while speaking with a patient; table