esson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #39 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"A sports medicine specialist determines that a hot-weather training strategy is appropriate for this athlete. To the nearest tenth, what can the mass of the athlete be for the training strategy to be appropriate?\\n\\n[IMAGE: A female runner finishing a race. Labels point to her: \\\"Finish\\\", bib number \\\"1789\\\", height \\\"165 cm\\\", and a note \\\"BSA < 2.0\\\".]\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"IMAGE PLACEHOLDER [illustration]: A female runner finishing a race. Labels point to her: \\\"Finish\\\", bib number \\\"1789\\\", height \\\"165 cm\\\", and a note \\\"BSA < 2.0\\\".\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 413: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q40\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #40 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Specialists can determine the speed a vehicle was traveling from the length of its skid marks, d, and the coefficient of friction, f. Rewrite the formula to solve for the length of the skid marks.\\n\\n\\\\placeholder{illustration}{A car leaving skid marks on a curvy road. A text box says: \\\"Vehicle's speed is 15.9\u221a(df).\\\"}\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 414: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q41\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #41 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Make Sense and Persevere The half-life of a certain type of soft drink is 5 h. If you drink 50 mL of this drink, the formula y = 50(0.5)^{t/5} tells the amount of the drink left in your system after t hours. How much of the soft drink will be left in your system after 16 hours?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 415: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q42\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #42 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Model With Mathematics Big Ben's pendulum takes 4 s to swing back and forth. The formula t = 2\u03c0\u221a((L)/(32)) gives the swing time, t, in seconds, based on the length of the pendulum, L, in feet. What is the minimum length necessary to build a clock with a pendulum that takes longer than Big Ben's pendulum to swing back and forth?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 416: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q43\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #43 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Make Sense and Persevere Derek is hang gliding on a clear day. Use the formula for visibility, v = 1.225\u221a(a) to find the altitude at which Derek is hang gliding.\\n\\n[IMAGE: A person hang gliding. Labels say: \\\"visibility, v = 67.1 miles\\\" and \\\"Distance to ground = a feet\\\".]\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"IMAGE PLACEHOLDER [illustration]: A person hang gliding. Labels say: \\\"visibility, $v = 67.1$ miles\\\" and \\\"Distance to ground = $a$ feet\\\".\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 417: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q44\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #44 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"table\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Complete the table to solve for the unknown value in the equation y = \\\\sqrt[3]{2x + z} - 12, using the given values in each row.\\n\\n\\\\begin{center}\\nTABLE:\\n y | x | z \\n\\n0 | 462 | -3 | 439 \\n\\n-10 | 1.25 | 3 | 16\\nEND_TABLE\\n\\\\end{center}\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 418: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q45\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #45 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"SAT/ACT What is the solution to the equation (x^2 + 5x + 25)^{3/2} = 343?\\n\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n \\\\item[A] -8 only\\n \\\\item[B] 3 only\\n \\\\item[C] 77 only\\n \\\\item[D] -8 and 3\\n \\\\item[E] There are no solutions.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 419: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q46\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #46 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"photo\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": true, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Performance Task Escape velocity is the velocity at which an object must be traveling to leave a star or planet without falling back to its surface or into orbit. Escape velocity depends on the gravitational constant, G = 6.67 \u00d7 10^{-11}, the mass, M in kilograms, and radius, r, of the star or planet.\\n\\n\\\\placeholder{illustration}{Earth with a rocket trajectory illustrating escape velocity. Labels: \\\"Escape velocity: v = \u221a((2GM)/(r))\\\" and \\\"Earth's radius = 6,371,000 m\\\".}\\n\\nPart A Rewrite the equation to solve for mass.\\n\\nPart B Earth has an escape velocity of about 11,200 meters per second. What is Earth's mass in kilograms?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 420: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-q16\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #16 (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Look for Relationships Write a radical equation that relates a square's perimeter to its area. Explain your reasoning. Use (s) to represent the side length of the square.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer