ible solution methods, how do you decide which to use?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 1, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 1 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-concept\", \"reference\", \"quick-guide\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 461: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-concept-summary-lesson-5-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Concept Summary (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solving Radical Equations\\n\\nTABLE:\\n p{3.5cm} p{4cm} p{4cm}}\\n\u2192prule | WORDS | ALGEBRA | GRAPH \\n\\\\midrule\\nStep 1 | Isolate the radical term. | 2\u221a(x + 3) - x = 0 2\u221a(x + 3) = x | \\\\multirow{4}{*}{\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n} \\nStep 2 | Square both sides to remove the radical. | (2\u221a(x + 3))^2 = (x)^2 | Step 3 | Solve the equation. | 4(x + 3) = x^2 x^2 - 4x - 12 = 0 (x - 6)(x + 2) = 0 x = 6 or x = -2 | Step 4 | Eliminate extraneous solutions. | 2\u221a(6 + 3) - 6 \\\\stackrel{?}{=} 0 2\u221a(9) \\\\stackrel{?}{=} 6 6 \\\\stackrel{?}{=} 6 6 = 6 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{green}{\\\\checkmark}} 2\u221a(-2 + 3) - (-2) \\\\stackrel{?}{=} 0 2\u221a(1) \\\\stackrel{?}{=} -2 2 \\\\stackrel{?}{=} -2 2 \u2260 -2 \\\\ \\\\text{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\ding{55}}} | \\\\bottomrule\\nEND_TABLE\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-concept\", \"concept-summary\", \"share-summary\", \"reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Solving Radical Equations  TABLE:  p{3.5cm} p{4cm} p{4cm}} \u2192prule | WORDS | ALGE' \u2192 [identify-extraneous-solution, solve-radical-equation, isolate-radical]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-radical-equation\", \"isolate-radical\", \"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 462: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-concept-summary-lesson-5-4-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Concept Summary (lesson 5-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Solving Radical Equations\\n\\nTABLE:\\n p{0.23\\\\linewidth}p{0.37\\\\linewidth}p{0.24\\\\linewidth}}\\n\u2192prule | Words | Algebra | Graph \\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\nStep 1 | Isolate the radical term. | (2\u221a(x+3)-x=0) (2\u221a(x+3)=x) | \\\\\\nStep 2 | Square both sides to remove the radical. | ((2\u221a(x+3))^2=(x)^2) | \\\\\\nStep 3 | Solve the equation. | (4(x+3)=x^2) (x^2-4x-12=0) ((x-6)(x+2)=0) (x=6 or x=-2) | [GRAPH / TIKZ figure] \\\\\\nStep 4 | Eliminate extraneous solutions. | (2\u221a(6+3)=6) (2\u221a(9)=6) (6=6) (2\u221a(-2+3)=-2) (2\u221a(1)=-2) (2\\\\ne -2) | \\\\\\n\\\\bottomrule\\nEND_TABLE\\n\\nDo You UNDERSTAND?\\n\\n1. Essential Question What properties of exponents allow you to solve equations that include radicals or rational exponents?\\n \\n\\n2. Construct Arguments How can you use a graph to show that the solution to (\\\\sqrt[3]{84x+8}=8) is 6?\\n \\n\\n3. Vocabulary Why does solving a radical equation sometimes result in an extraneous solution?\\n \\n\\n4. Error Analysis Nazim said that (-3) and 6 are the solutions to (\u221a(3x+18)=x). What error did Nazim make?\\n \\n\\n5. Communicate Precisely Describe how you would solve the equation (x^{(2)/(3)}=n). How is this solution method to be interpreted if the equation had been written in radical form instead?\\n \\n\\nDo You KNOW HOW?\\n\\nSolve for (x).\\n\\n6. (3\u221a(x+22)=21)\\n \\n\\n7. (\\\\sqrt[3]{5x}=25)\\n \\n\\nIn 8 and 9, find the extraneous solution.\\n\\n8. (\u221a(8x+9)=x)\\n \\n\\n9. (x=\u221a(24-2x))\\n \\n\\n10. Rewrite the equation (y=\u221a((x-48)/(6))) to isolate (x).\\n \\n\\n11. Use a graph to find the solution to the equation (9=\u221a(3x+11)).\\n \\n\\nSolve each equation.\\n\\n12. ((3x+2)^{(2)/(5)}=4)\\n \\n\\n13. (\u221a(2x-5)-\u221a(x-3)=1)\\n \\n\\n14. (\u221a(x+2)+\u221a(3x+4)=2)\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"savvas-concept\", \"concept-summary\", \"share-summary\", \"reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 463: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-learntogether-les\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 LearnTogether (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Instructional Focus Students explore solving an equation with exponents using two different methods. This prepares students to solve equations with radicals. Students learn that the methods used to solve equations with exponents and radicals are similar.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"learn-together\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Instructional Focus Students explore solving an equation with exponents using tw' \u2192 [solve-radical-equation]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 464: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-generalizeetp-les\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 GeneralizeETP (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Before: Implement Tasks that Promote Reasoning and Problem Solving.\\n\\nQ: What do you notice about the equations in Parts A and B?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"generalize-etp\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Each equation contains the same integers and operations. However, in part A, you find ((a+1)^2), whereas in Part B, you find ((a+1)).\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 465: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-generalizeetp-les-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 GeneralizeETP (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"During: Support Productive Struggle in Learning Mathematics.\\n\\nQ: How can your knowledge of solving equations with exponents assist you in solving equations with radicals?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"generalize-etp\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): In order to isolate the expression with the variable, you need to perform the opposite operation. This means if the expression has a square root, you need to square it to isolate the variable.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 466: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-generalizeetp-les-3\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 GeneralizeETP (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"For Early Finishers\\n\\nQ: Solve (5(x-2)^4-7=73) and (5\\\\sqrt[4]{x-2}-7=73). Which solution method did you choose?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"generalize-etp\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): (x=0,4); (x=65{,}538); Each equation can be solved algebraically in just a few steps. It would be difficult to solve these equations by graphing because the numbers get very large.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 467: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-generalizeetp-les-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 GeneralizeETP (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"After: Facilitate Meaningful Mathematical Discourse.\\n\\nQ: What methods can you use to solve an equation with a radical and an equation with an exponent?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"generalize-etp\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): For each type of equation, you can isolate the variables by performing the opposite operations. You can also graph the two equations created from the original equation.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 468: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-habitsofmind-less\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 HabitsOfMind (lesson 5-4, anchor lesson-level)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Construct Arguments The squares of two numbers are equal. Does that mean that the two numbers themselves must also be equal? Explain.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex0-te\", \"habits-of-mind\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): No; two numbers that have the same value but opposite signs have the same square.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"construct-argument\"]}\n 469: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-learntogether-les-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 LearnTogether (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 1)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Establish Mathematics Goals to Focus Learning. Introduce students to solving equations with radicals and rational exponents. Remind them that, just as they used properties of exponents to solve equations with exponents, they can use these properties to solve equations with radicals.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex1-te\", \"learn-together\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 470: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 1)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: What does it mean to isolate the radical?\\n\\nQ: How do you eliminate the square root or cube root? Why do you want to eliminate them?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex1-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Rewrite the equation so the radical is alone on one side of the equation.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-zeros\", \"isolate-radical\", \"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 471: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-elicitevidence-le\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 ElicitEvidence (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 1)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: How do you know whether to square or cube the radical when solving for (x)?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex1-te\", \"elicit-evidence\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): If the equation contains a square root, then square both sides to eliminate the radical. If the equation contains a cube root, then cube both sides to eliminate the radical.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": []}\n 472: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 2)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: Why is the equation solved for (y)?\\n\\nQ: Why are parentheses placed around (0.010583x) in the third step?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex2-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): The equation is solved for (y) so you can find the height of the suspension cable more easily when you know your distance from the middle of the bridge.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 473: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-commonerror-lesso\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 CommonError (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 2)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Try It! 2 After dividing by the constant, some students may forget to square both the variable and the constant, which would result in ((v^2)/(3.57)=d) instead of ((v^2)/(12.74)=d). Have students make sure the entire equation has been squared, including 3.57.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex2-te\", \"common-error\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: 'Try It! 2 After dividing by the constant, some students may forget to square bot' \u2192 [solve-radical-equation]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 474: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-habitsofmind-less-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 HabitsOfMind (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 2)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Reason Reese solved the equation in Try It 1a by first squaring both sides. Is this an appropriate first step? Why or why not?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex2-te\", \"habits-of-mind\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): No; you must first subtract 3 from both sides of the equation to isolate the radical. Then you can square both sides.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 475: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-elladdendum-lesso\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 ELLAddendum (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 2)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"SPEAKING BEGINNING In small groups, have students isolate an object from a group of objects. Have students discuss what the term isolate means (to set apart or to be alone). If they have not heard the word before, challenge them to use it in a sentence based on the definition given.\\n\\nQ: When have you heard the word, or a form of the word, isolate used?\\n\\nQ: How are your answers to the last question related to isolating the variable (y) in the example?\\n\\nREADING DEVELOPING Ask students to read the first sentence of the example and read the definition of suspend from the dictionary.\\n\\nQ: Can you identify the root word in the word suspension?\\n\\nQ: What does suspend mean?\\n\\nQ: Have the definitions of suspension and suspension bridge on index cards. Hand out the cards and have students read the definitions aloud. How do the definitions help you to understand the meaning of suspension cables mentioned in the example?\\n\\nLISTENING EXPANDING Have students listen as you read the following sentences aloud. 1) He wore a pair of glasses on the bridge of his nose. 2) The isthmus is a land bridge between the two countries. 3) The interpreter is a bridge between people who speak different languages.\\n\\nQ: What does the word bridge indicate in each of these examples?\\n\\nQ: The example describes the physical structure of a bridge. Does a bridge always have to be a structure that you physically cross over? Explain.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex2-te\", \"elladdendum\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): The prisoner was put in solitary isolation. The patient with an infectious disease must be isolated, even from his family.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-zeros\", \"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 476: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-3\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"PART A\\n\\nQ: Why can you find potential solutions to an equation that are not actual solutions of the original equation?\\n\\nPART B\\n\\nQ: When solving an equation, what indicates that there may be an extraneous solution?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex3-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Squaring both sides of an equation may create a new equation with two solutions. Only one of these solutions may satisfy the original equation.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"distractor-analysis\"]}\n 477: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-elicitevidence-le-2\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 ElicitEvidence (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: In part (b), why do you need to be careful when squaring both sides of the equation?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex3-te\", \"elicit-evidence\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): You need to make sure that you square the entire left side, ((x+2)). This will require applying the Distributive Property to multiply the two binomials.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 478: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 3)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Example 3 Help students understand how to use what they know about radical equations to solve a system consisting of a linear and a radical equation.\\n\\nQ: How can you use the graph to predict whether there will be any extraneous solutions?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex3-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): The graphs of the functions intersect at 1 point, so I expect to find 1 solution when I solve the equation. If I find 2 solutions, it is likely that 1 will be extraneous.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 479: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-5\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"PART A\\n\\nQ: Why do you raise each side of an equation to the reciprocal power?\\n\\nPART B\\n\\nQ: Why did the right side of the equation change from ((x-2)^3) to ((x-2)^2)?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex4-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Multiplying a rational exponent by its reciprocal equals 1.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 480: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-elicitevidence-le-3\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 ElicitEvidence (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: Why do you isolate the expression with the rational exponent?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex4-te\", \"elicit-evidence\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): When you isolate the expression with the rational exponent, only the expression will remain after each side of the equation is raised to the reciprocal power.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-for-variable\"]}\n 481: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-rtiextend-lesson-\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 RtIExtend (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Use with Example 4 Have students explore solving equations with rational exponents that have no solution by graphing. Students should recognize that (\\\\sqrt{x}) is equal to an expression raised to the ((1)/(2)) power.\\n\\nSolve the equation (\u221a(6x+8)=x-7).\\n\\nQ: What strategy can you use to solve this equation?\\n\\nQ: What do you recognize about the solution?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex4-te\", \"rt-iextend\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Sample: graphing\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-rational-exponent-equation\"]}\n 482: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-6\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: What is another strategy you could use to find the solution to an equation without doing calculations?\\n\\nQ: Why does a radical remain in the solution steps after the two sides of the equation have been squared the first time?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex5-te\", \"purposeful-questions\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): You could graph the two sides of the equation separately. The solution is where the graphs intersect.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 483: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-elicitevidence-le-4\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 ElicitEvidence (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Q: What method can you use to solve equations with two radicals?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex5-te\", \"elicit-evidence\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Isolate the radicals on either side of the equation and square both sides. Then the first equation can be solved by factoring, but the second equation must be solved using the Quadratic Formula.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 484: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-habitsofmind-less-3\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 HabitsOfMind (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Reason Why are extraneous solutions a possibility for radical equations?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex5-te\", \"habits-of-mind\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Extraneous solutions can occur when you square both sides of the equation.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-radical-equation\"]}\n 485: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-rtisupport-lesson\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 RtISupport (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Use with Example 5 Some students may struggle with squaring each side of an equation when solving an equation with two radicals. Have students practice squaring expressions.\\n\\nSquare the following expressions.\\n\\nQ: (12)\\n\\nQ: (\u221a(5x))\\n\\nQ: (\u221a(x)+6)\\n\\nQ: (\u221a(3x)+10)\\n\\nQ: What is important to remember when squaring these expressions?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-5-4\", \"teacher-edition\", \"ex5-te\", \"rt-isupport\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): 144\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\", \"A-CED.A.4\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"distractor-analysis\"]}\n 486: {\"id\": \"5-4-savvas-teacher-edition-purposefulquestio-7\", \"lesson\": \"5-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Teacher Edition \u2014 PurposefulQuestions (lesson 5-4, anchor Example 6)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visu