ated_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"F-LE.A.4\", \"A-REI.D.11\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"6-3-savvas-example-3-lesson-6-3\"], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [\"3-5-savvas-q30\", \"5-4-savvas-q41\"], \"skill_tokens\": [\"solve-exponential-equation\", \"model-continuous-compound\", \"extract-time-from-model\"]}\n 669: {\"id\": \"6-3-savvas-q41\", \"lesson\": \"6-3\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #41 (lesson 6-3, anchors Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Use a calculator to evaluate each expression. Round to the nearest ten-thousandth. SEE EXAMPLE 4\\n\\n\\\\log 0.24\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 1, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-1 item anchored to Example 4. Single-step recall/recognition.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-6-3\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-4-anchor\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): -0.6198\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 672: {\"id\": \"6-3-savvas-q44\", \"lesson\": \"6-3\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #44 (lesson 6-3, anchors Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Use a calculator to evaluate each expression. Round to the nearest ten-thousandth. SEE EXAMPLE 4\\n\\n\\\\log 12\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 1, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-1 item anchored to Example 4. Single-step recall/recognition.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-6-3\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-4-anchor\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): 1.0792\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\"}\n 740: {\"id\": \"6-4-savvas-q10\", \"lesson\": \"6-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #10 (lesson 6-4, anchors Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Communicate Precisely How is the graph of the logarithmic function g(x)=\\\\log_2(x-7) related to the graph of the function f(x)=\\\\log_2 x? Explain your reasoning.\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item anchored to Example 4. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-6-4\", \"savvas-practice\", \"example-4-anchor\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): g(x) is shifted 7 units to the right; for each x, g(x)=f(x-7), so the y-values of the functions are equal for x-values that are 7 units apart.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"F-IF.C.7e\", \"F-BF.B.3\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"evaluate-log\", \"graph-log-function\"]}\n 814: {\"id\": \"6-5-savvas-q10\", \"lesson\": \"6-5\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #10 (lesson 6-5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Communicate Precisely Saachi used the Change of Base Formula to solve the equation 6^x=72 and found that x=2.387. How can Saachi check her solution?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-6-5\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Answer key (from source): Saachi can check if 6^{2.387}=72.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-20\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"F-LE.A.4\", \"F-BF.B.5\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"change-of-base\"]}\n 903: {\"id\": \"4-4-savvas-q10\", \"lesson\": \"4-4\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #10 (lesson 4-4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"(9y + 2)/(3y^2 - 2y - 8) + (7)/(3y^2 + y - 4)\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Savvas-declared DOK-2 item. Routine multi-step procedure.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-4\", \"savvas-practice\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"T1 pool: '(9y + 2)/(3y^2 - 2y - 8) + (7)/(3y^2 + y - 4)' \u2192 [add-rational-expressions]\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-21\", \"role\": \"explore-practice\", \"standards\": [\"A-APR.D.7\", \"A-SSE.A.2\"], \"prereq_ids\": [], \"rehearses\": [], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"add-rational-expressions\"]}\n 975: {\"id\": \"4-5-savvas-example-3-lesson-4-5\", \"lesson\": \"4-5\", \"source\": \"Savvas Example 3 (lesson 4-5)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": true, \"visual_type\": \"graph\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Identify an Extraneous Solution\\n\\nWhat is the solution of the equation (1)/(x-5) + (x)/(x-3) = (2)/(x^2-8x+15)?\\n\\nStep 1 Multiply each side of the equation by the common denominator, (x-5)(x-3).\\n\\n(x-5)(x-3)((1)/(x-5) + (x)/(x-3)) = (2(x-5)(x-3))/(x^2-8x+15)\\n\\nStep 2 Continue to simplify.\\n\\n((x-5)(x-3))/(x-5) + (x(x-5)(x-3))/(x-3) = (2(x-5)(x-3))/(x^2-8x+15)\\n\\nStep 3 Divide out common factors in the numerator and the denominator.\\n\\nStep 4 Solve the equation.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(x-3) + x(x-5) &= 2 \\nx - 3 + x^2 - 5x &= 2 \\nx^2 - 4x - 3 &= 2 \\nx^2 - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\n(x-5)(x+1) &= 0 \\nx = 5 & and x = -1\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\\nNote: You can divide out common factors under the assumption that ((x-5))/((x-5)) = 1 and ((x-3))/((x-3)) = 1. This is only true if x \u2260 5 or 3. \\nNote: Consider both solutions. If either solution makes the value of the denominator 0, it is not valid.\\n\\nThe solution x = 5 is an extraneous solution because it is not in the domain of the original equation.\\n\\nThe solution of the equation (1)/(x-5) + (x)/(x-3) = (2)/(x^2-8x+15) is -1.\\n\\nTo see that there is only one solution, graph f(x) = (1)/(x-5) + (x)/(x-3) and g(x) = (2)/(x^2-8x+15).\\n\\n\\\\begin{center}\\n[GRAPH / TIKZ figure]\\n\\\\end{center}\\n\\nBoth graphs have vertical asymptotes at x = 3 and x = 5. Therefore, neither graph has a value at x = 5. The graphs only intersect in one point, at x = -1.\\n\\nCONCEPTUAL UNDERSTANDING: LOOK FOR RELATIONSHIPS How are an extraneous solution and an asymptote related? Is this always true?\", \"answers\": [], \"correct\": null, \"dok\": 2, \"dok_rationale\": \"Auto-assigned DOK 2 from source structure. Verify against Item Analysis.\", \"topics\": [], \"tags\": [\"lesson-4-5\", \"savvas-example\", \"example-3\", \"teacher-reference\"], \"used_in\": [], \"notes\": \"Launch. EXTRANEOUS SOLUTIONS \u2014 the signature move of 4-5. Previews 5-4's parallel move in radical equations.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-04-21\", \"role\": \"launch-model-2\", \"standards\": [\"A-REI.A.2\"], \"prereq_ids\": [\"4-5-savvas-example-1-lesson-4-5\"], \"rehearses\": [\"4-5-savvas-try-it-3-lesson-4-5\", \"4-5-savvas-q10\"], \"echoes\": [], \"skill_tokens\": [\"clear-denominators\", \"identify-extraneous-solution\", \"solve-rational-equation\"]}\n 992: {\"id\": \"4-5-savvas-q10\", \"lesson\": \"4-5\", \"source\": \"Savvas Practice #10 (lesson 4-5, anchors Example 4)\", \"page\": null, \"image\": null, \"has_visual\": false, \"visual_type\": \"none\", \"visual_needs_cleanup\": false, \"visual_clean_asset\": null, \"prompt\": \"Error Analysis Describe and correct the error Miranda made in solving the rational equation.\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n(1)/(x-2) + (x-2)/(x+2) &= (x-4)/(x-2) \\n(x+2)(x-2)((1)/(x-2) + (x-2)/(x+2)) &= ((x-4)/(x-2))(x+2)(x-2) \\n(x+2)(1) + (x-2)(x-2) &= (x+4)(x+2) \\nx + 2 + x^2 - 4x + 4 &= x^2 + 6x + 8 \\n-3x + 6 &= 6x + 8 \\n-2 &= 9x; or x = -(2)/(9) X\\n\\\\end{align*}\", \